        <div id="taste-mixing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. What Do Buyers Say About Taste and Mixing?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buyers mostly praise MuscleTech's taste and mixing, and the most common complaint is price, followed by tubs that look half empty. Nitro-Tech shows a 4.5-star average across roughly 20,000 reviews aggregated on MuscleTech's site from its own store, Amazon and Walmart at the time of writing. We have not tasted these products; this section reports what buyers say.
          </p>

          <p>
            On Nitro-Tech, reviewers frequently describe Cookies and Cream as tasting like a milkshake and Strawberry as tasting like strawberry milk, and several say it mixes without clumps in a shaker, especially with cold liquid. Milk Chocolate is the default most people start with. On Grass-Fed, buyers on MuscleTech's site praise the chocolate and say it sits well on the stomach, and one notes buying it at Sam's Club for the size. On 100% Mass Gainer, some buyers find the vanilla very sweet and rich.
          </p>

          <p>
            The recurring complaints are predictable. Several reviewers say Nitro-Tech is expensive at full price and worth it only on sale. Others notice that the tub is not full to the top; MuscleTech's FAQ answers this directly, saying powders are filled by weight, not volume, and settle in transit, and that the stated number of servings is what you get. People who avoid artificial sweeteners will find the Nitro-Tech line sweet, since it uses both sucralose and acesulfame potassium.
          </p>

          <p>
            If sweetness bothers you, Grass-Fed 100% Whey is the MuscleTech tub without artificial sweeteners, or look at our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-artificial-sweeteners/">protein powder without artificial sweeteners</a> list. If you have a sensitive stomach, the concentrate-led blends and the gum blends in Nitro-Tech and Whey Gold may be the cause; an isolate is the usual fix.
          </p>
        </div>

