        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Who Should Buy MuscleTech, and Who Should Skip It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            MuscleTech suits budget-minded lifters who want a familiar, sweet-tasting whey, and it suits creatine users best of all. Nitro-Tech is one of the few big-brand wheys that puts a real 3 g creatine dose in every scoop, which saves buying and measuring a second powder. Whey Gold is a sound plain whey at a fair price, and Ripped is worth a look whenever its price undercuts the rest of the range.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip MuscleTech if you compete in a drug-tested sport, since we found no NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or Informed Choice claim for these tubs. Skip it if you want no artificial sweeteners (except the Grass-Fed tub), if you need a pure isolate for lactose, or if you want the cleanest possible ingredient list; the Nitro-Tech line has gums, carrageenan, lecithin and added flavors.
          </p>

          <p>
            A few health notes apply. Creatine is well studied in healthy adults, but people with kidney disease should check with a doctor before taking it daily. All these tubs contain milk, and most contain soy. Pregnant or breastfeeding readers should get medical advice before using Nitro-Tech Ripped, which contains green coffee extract and kelp. Older adults with medical conditions should run any new supplement past a doctor or registered dietitian.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are still deciding between brands, our <a href="/best-whey-protein-powder/">best whey protein powders</a> roundup ranks the field on label, testing and cost per 25 g, and today's <a href="/pure-protein-vs-premier-protein/">Pure Protein vs Premier Protein</a> comparison covers two more budget names you will see on the same shelves.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming all 30 g in Nitro-Tech is whey</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Nitro-Tech's panel lists added L-leucine, L-valine and L-isoleucine alongside the whey, and it does not say how much of the 30 g they account for. The whole-whey content is probably a little lower. That is fine for most people, but compare it with plain 24 g wheys knowing the numbers are not perfectly like-for-like.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating cGMP as third-party testing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                MuscleTech's "Quality Tested" line refers to cGMP manufacturing, which every US supplement maker must follow, plus HPLC testing of its creatine. Neither is a finished-product banned-substance screen. Tested athletes need a product listed in the NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport database, and we found no such claim for these tubs.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Doubling up on creatine by accident</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Nitro-Tech already supplies 3 g of creatine per scoop, and MuscleTech's gainers carry 5 g per serving. If you also take a separate creatine scoop, or drink two or three Nitro-Tech shakes a day, you can land at 9 to 12 g, well beyond what research uses for daily maintenance. Count every source.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the 2 lb tub to save money</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The small tub looks cheaper at the register but costs more per gram. At the prices we saw, 2 lb Nitro-Tech worked out to about $2.13 per 25 g against about $1.57 for the 4 lb tub, roughly a third more. If you will finish it within a few months, the larger size is the better buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a mass gainer as your protein powder</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A full serving of 100% Mass Gainer is 1,330 calories and about $3.57 per 25 g of protein, mostly paid for carbohydrate. If you only want more protein, Nitro-Tech or Whey Gold does that at less than half the cost per gram. Use a gainer only when you genuinely struggle to eat enough calories.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

