              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  MuscleTech says its products are made to cGMP standards and its creatine is HPLC-tested, and it reports that 100% Mass Gainer showed no detectable lead in Consumer Reports' October 2025 testing. We found no NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or Informed Choice claim on its protein pages at the time of writing, so drug-tested athletes should choose a certified product.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein is in MuscleTech Nitro-Tech?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Nitro-Tech lists 30 g of protein per 46 g scoop of Milk Chocolate, per MuscleTech's label at the time of writing, with 3 g of creatine monohydrate and 160 calories. Vanilla Cream is a 45 g scoop with 150 calories. Some of the 30 g figure includes added free leucine, valine and isoleucine, and the label does not split the amounts.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is MuscleTech protein healthy and clean?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  MuscleTech is a standard mainstream whey: a fully disclosed label with no proprietary blends, 2 g of sugar and 0 g added sugar per Nitro-Tech scoop. It is not a clean-label brand, though. Nitro-Tech uses sucralose, acesulfame potassium, carrageenan and other gums. Grass-Fed 100% Whey is the exception, with no artificial sweeteners, flavors or colors per MuscleTech.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does MuscleTech contain artificial sweeteners or lactose?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Most MuscleTech proteins contain artificial sweeteners: Nitro-Tech and Ripped list sucralose and acesulfame potassium, and Whey Gold lists sucralose with either acesulfame potassium or steviol glycosides depending on the listing. All of them are milk-based with whey concentrate listed first, so they contain some lactose. Grass-Fed is free of artificial sweeteners, per MuscleTech.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which MuscleTech flavor is best?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Buyer reviews most often single out Nitro-Tech Cookies and Cream, which several reviewers describe as tasting like a milkshake, and Milk Chocolate as the safe default. Strawberry gets praise for tasting like strawberry milk. For Grass-Fed, buyers mostly mention Triple Chocolate. We have not tasted them; this reflects what reviewers report on MuscleTech's site.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What are the common complaints about MuscleTech?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  The most common complaints are full price, with many buyers saying they wait for sales, and tubs that look half empty on opening. MuscleTech says its powders are filled by weight and settle in transit. Some buyers find the Nitro-Tech line and the vanilla gainer too sweet, and people sensitive to lactose may notice the concentrate-led blend.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Nitro-Tech better than Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Nitro-Tech is better if you want creatine included: 30 g of protein plus 3 g of creatine per scoop, against 24 g and no creatine in Gold Standard. Gold Standard is better for tested athletes and lactose-sensitive buyers, since it carries Informed Choice per ON and lists isolate first. On cost per 25 g they sit within a few cents of each other.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
